\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Mass coefficients at different fine depths}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{A [line of constant physics](fdip-01D3) has coefficient
#{t\varepsilon^{-K}} at fine depth #{K}. Blocking to a fixed
depth #{k} multiplies it by #{\varepsilon^{K-k}}, giving
##{\varepsilon^{K-k}t\varepsilon^{-K}=t\varepsilon^{-k}.}
For #{0<\lambda<1}, the identity and the family of top
projectors both have multiplier #{\lambda^{-1}} by
\ref{fdip-01CR}. Their coefficients #{\mu\lambda^K}
and #{m\lambda^K} give the mass in \ref{fdip-01CK} and
the top coefficient of the [fixed-ratio precision](fdip-00BY).
At #{\lambda\ge1} these families still exist, but the
directions are not relevant.}

\p{On the cycle, the exact decimation coordinate of
\ref{fdip-00AY} is #{\eta}, with #{\eta\mapsto q\eta}
under decimation by #{q}. Taking #{\eta=m/N} fixes its
value at any fixed coarse size; the shape is #{\cosh(m/N)}.
For the grid precisions in \ref{fdip-01D4}, the lattice
mass is #{\mu_N/N^2}. When #{\mu_N\to\mu}, this is
an asymptotic version of the same rule. It equals the fixed
coefficient #{\mu /N^2} only when #{\mu_N=\mu}.
The factor #{N^2} compares length scales as in \ref{fdip-004K}.}
